<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">1716952</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">1977675</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>c-src structure in human cancers with elevated pp60c-src activity.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Fromowitz</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Koslow</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hagag</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Viola</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Department of Medicine, State University of New York, Stony Brook 11794.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>9</month><year>1991</year></pub-date><volume>64</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>531</fpage><lpage>533</lpage><abstract><p>We used RNAase protection and restriction fragment length polymorphism assays to detect activating mutations of c-src in a spectrum of human tumours. No mutations were detected at codons 98, 381, 444, and 530. We conclude that mutational activation is not the mechanism of enhancement of pp60c-src-specific kinase activity found in a number of human cancer types.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>Figure 2</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0122-a" xlink:role="532"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Figure 3</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0122-b" xlink:role="532"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>Figure 4</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0122-c" xlink:role="532"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0121.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="531" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0122.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="532" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00073-0123.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="533" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>